The Nautilus DevOps team is strategizing the migration of a portion of their infrastructure to the AWS cloud. Recognizing the scale of this undertaking, they have opted to approach the migration in incremental steps rather than as a single massive transition. To achieve this, they have segmented large tasks into smaller, more manageable units.

For this task, create an EC2 instance using Terraform with the following requirements:

The name of the instance must be xfusion-ec2-t1q2.

Use the Amazon Linux ami-0c101f26f147fa7fd to launch this instance.

The Instance type must be t2.micro.

Create a new RSA key named xfusion-kp-t1q2.

Attach the default (available by default) security group.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform/t1q2. Create the main.tf file (do not create a different .tf file) to provision the instance.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.
SOLution
Here is the main.tf for the /home/bob/terraform/t1q2 working directory. It creates the RSA key pair, looks up the default security group, and provisions the instance with all the specified requirements.

📄 /home/bob/terraform/t1q2/main.tf
hcl
# Generate a new RSA private key
resource "tls_private_key" "xfusion_kp" {
  algorithm = "RSA"
  rsa_bits  = 4096
}

# Register the public key as an AWS key pair
resource "aws_key_pair" "xfusion_kp" {
  key_name   = "xfusion-kp-t1q2"
  public_key = tls_private_key.xfusion_kp.public_key_openssh
}

# Look up the default VPC
data "aws_vpc" "default" {
  default = true
}

# Look up the default security group in the default VPC
data "aws_security_group" "default" {
  vpc_id = data.aws_vpc.default.id
  name   = "default"
}

# Create the EC2 instance
resource "aws_instance" "xfusion_ec2" {
  ami                    = "ami-0c101f26f147fa7fd"
  instance_type          = "t2.micro"
  key_name               = aws_key_pair.xfusion_kp.key_name
  vpc_security_group_ids = [data.aws_security_group.default.id]

  tags = {
    Name = "xfusion-ec2-t1q2"
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Instance Name: Name = "xfusion-ec2-t1q2" tag — this is what identifies the instance in AWS.

AMI: ami-0c101f26f147fa7fd (Amazon Linux) as specified.

Instance Type: t2.micro.

RSA Key Pair: tls_private_key generates a 4096-bit RSA key locally, and aws_key_pair registers its public portion in AWS with the name xfusion-kp-t1q2.

Default Security Group: The data blocks look up the default VPC and its default security group, then attach it via vpc_security_group_ids.

Region: Handled by provider.tf. Since the working directory is now /home/bob/terraform/t1q2, make sure provider.tf is present in that directory. If it isn't, copy it from the parent:

bash
cp /home/bob/terraform/provider.tf /home/bob/terraform/t1q2/
🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform/t1q2
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Confirm No Changes
bash
terraform plan
It should return:

text
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
🔍 Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=xfusion-ec2-t1q2" \
  --query "Reservations[].Instances[].{ID:InstanceId,Type:InstanceType,Key:KeyName,State:State.Name}"
You should see the instance with type t2.micro, key name xfusion-kp-t1q2, and state running (or pending briefly).

⚠️ Note on the Working Directory
The task specifies /home/bob/terraform/t1q2 (a subdirectory). Terraform only reads .tf files from the current working directory, not parent directories. So either:

Ensure provider.tf is copied into t1q2/, or

Add the provider block directly into main.tf (if no provider.tf exists there).

